# Build context for infra/docker/*.Dockerfile is the repo root (see
# infra/docker-compose.yml build.context: ..). Keep VCS/harness bookkeeping,
# language caches, and local envs out of build images.
#
# Every pattern below is anchored at the CONTEXT ROOT unless it starts with `**/`.
# That is a real trap in this file's history: a bare `__pycache__` excluded
# `./__pycache__` and nothing else, so `backend/adapters/db/migrations/__pycache__`
# still went into the context and buildkit failed the whole build on it —
#   ERROR: invalid file request backend/.../__pycache__/env.cpython-312.pyc
# The rule of thumb: if the thing can appear at more than one depth (a cache
# directory, an env file), it needs `**/`.
.git
.claude
ProductSpecification
infra/.memory-bank
infra/swarm-report
**/node_modules
# Local build/report output — the frontend image runs its own `npm run build` inside the
# Dockerfile, so shipping the host's dist/ or coverage/ only bloats the context (and trips
# buildkit's "invalid file request" on OneDrive-synced artifacts, which is exactly how
# frontend/coverage broke the build once dist/ had been fixed).
frontend/dist
frontend/coverage
.venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/htmlcov
**/coverage.xml
**/*.egg-info
# `.env` alone left every suffixed copy in the build context. A hand-made backup
# beside it -- `.env.bak-pre-key`, taken before a credential rotation -- would have
# been copied into an image layer, and a pushed image is a pushed secret that no
# .gitignore rule reaches. The example file is the one .env.* that is safe and
# useful to ship, so it is re-included explicitly.
#
# Recursive, for the same reason as the caches above and with more at stake: the
# root-anchored form left `backend/.env` — the file that holds the model credentials —
# in the context, and backend.Dockerfile copies the whole `backend/` tree. The
# containers get their env from compose's `env_file` at RUN time, so nothing needs
# these baked into a layer.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
